\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\taxon{Convention}
\title{Identifiability regimes for semantic representation diagnostics}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{evaluation}
\tag{identifiability}
\tag{representation}

\p{Zhou and Klindt study two causal data-generating directions, labels to
latents and latents to labels, with effective latent dimension
#{n'={\rm rank}(W^*)} and learned activation width #{m}. The source boundary
for this note is Theorems 4.1 to 4.4, Sections 3 to 5, and Section 6 of
\citet{Theorems 4.1 to 4.4, Sections 3 to 5, and Section 6}{zhou2026complete}.}

\p{\strong{Source observation.} When #{m\geq n'}, Theorem 4.1 makes the
classification-relevant part of every optimum linear, #{h(z)=Az+\nu(z)} with
#{UA=W^*}; the null-readout term #{\nu} may remain unconstrained. When
#{m<n'}, Theorem 4.2 gives generic nonlinear, non-identifiable optima under
its support and regularity hypotheses. These are different capacity regimes,
not two scores for one representation.}

\p{The stronger conclusions require additional structure. In the generative
case, the sparse non-negative model, exact capacity, non-negative activations,
fixed within-class variance, and an energy-minimizing optimum give Theorem
4.3's scaled permutation
#{h(z)=DP_\sigma z}. Each neuron then follows one latent coordinate under
those assumptions. In the discriminative case, Theorem 4.4 assumes
#{m\geq n'}, #{k}-sparse latents, and #{\operatorname{spark}(A^*)>2k}; the
classifier-visible encoder has the same null space as #{A^*}, so the unique
#{k}-sparse solution can recover #{z}. Axis alignment and sparse recovery are
distinct routes through distinct assumptions.}

\p{Section 5 reports finite experiments rather than a universal law. Linear
latent recovery is measured by supervised #{R^2}; permutation-and-scaling
alignment is measured by the mean correlation coefficient (MCC). Sparse
recovery compares supervised Lasso, dense OLS, and unsupervised sparse
dictionary learning, while cross-system correspondence uses the Maximum
Matching Unit (MMU) readout. The reported synthetic runs use ten seeds and
show the predicted capacity transition; sparse supervised Lasso recovers
latents more fully than dense OLS, while cross-system MIS and MMU associations
are finite empirical correlations. Section 6 records limits from constant
conditional entropy, non-negativity, class priors, and the assumed sparse data
generating process; signed latents do not inherit Theorem 4.3 directly.}

\p{\strong{Finite E2 record.} For a fixed model family, checkpoint, task split,
and probe seed, record}
##{
\mathcal D=\bigl(r,m,n',\mathcal A,[h]_{\sim_r},
R^2_{\rm lin},\operatorname{MCC}_{\rm axis},\operatorname{MMU}_{\rm cross},\mathsf E\bigr).
}
\p{Here #{r} is one declared regime: sufficient-capacity linear,
undercomplete nonlinear, generative axis-aligned, or discriminative sparse
recovery. #{\mathcal A} lists the causal direction and assumptions actually
held. The equivalence class #{[h]_{\sim_r}} identifies only the ambiguity
allowed by that regime: linear changes and null-readout freedom in the first,
the declared nonlinear class in the second, permutation and positive scaling
in the third, and the measured sparse-decoder or null-space class in the
fourth. The three readouts remain separate fields; an unavailable or
inapplicable field is #{\bot}. No scalar summary is defined, and none of
these fields alone establishes monosemanticity.}

\p{\strong{FTIP deduction.} This record makes E2's representation question
conditional on capacity, causal direction, and an explicit equivalence class.
It lets the existing checkpoint route report linear
recoverability, axis alignment, sparse recovery, and cross-system matching
without treating any one readout as a capability or deployment result. A
finite observation can support a regime-specific comparison; it cannot
identify a developmental mechanism or transfer beyond the declared task and
assumptions.}

\p{\strong{Proposed protocol.} Before inspecting a checkpoint, freeze the
task family, latent or proxy labels, split, width ratio, causal regime,
assumptions, decoder class, and held-out evaluator. Select layers or decoder
settings on validation data only. Report the full record, seed repetitions,
missingness, and assumption-violation arms; keep #{R^2}, MCC, and MMU in their
native units. For real model activations, treat latent recovery as a declared
proxy assay and report the proxy construction with the same boundary.}

\p{A scoped local formalization search found no declaration that packages this
four-regime record with its #{R^2}, MCC, and MMU readouts. Existing
representation and evaluation declarations cover neighboring fields, so this
finite E2 record remains a protocol proposal.}
